  "notes": "West leads the ♠8 against 3NT. When the ♠K holds at trick 1, it is apparent that West has led from a long suit headed by the ♠A. South has 8 top tricks (3 in hearts, the ♦A, and 3 in clubs). If clubs are 3-2, South will have more than enough tricks for his contract.\n\nThere is a danger only if East has 4 or more clubs to the ♣J. In this case, if South cashes the ♣A, ♣K, and ♣Q and surrenders a club trick to East, East will be able to play spades through South's ♠Q. West will be able to take enough spade tricks to set the contract.\n\nSouth can ensure the contract by playing a club from dummy to the ♣10 at trick 2. If the finesse works, South has his ninth trick immediately.\n\nIf West can win the ♣J, the contract is still safe. West cannot play spades profitably from his side of the table. South can later unblock the ♣A, cross to the ♥Q, and run dummy's clubs.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
